Privycs: serve the IPSec client identity from memory instead of the KeyChain.

charonservice.c binds getUserCertificate()/getUserKey() natively via a
hardcoded FindClass on the concrete CharonVpnService, and both are private on
it. There is no interface, no injection point and no subclassing path, so
patching this class is the only way to change what they return -- hence a
vendor patch for what is otherwise app-level behaviour.

The native layer only ever uses the key as an opaque signing oracle
(Signature.initSign; get_encoding is hard-wired to fail), so any PrivateKey
that initSign accepts satisfies it -- including one parsed in-process from the
PKCS#12 embedded in the profile's .sswan. That removes the KeyChain install,
which matters because Android exposes no API to delete an installed KeyChain
credential: the app could create one but never clean it up.

The identity itself lives in PrivycsCredentials, a Privycs-owned class in this
same package but NOT in this submodule (it is under
android/strongswan-lib/src/main/java/, merged into one compilation by
build.gradle.kts). Keeping the state and lifecycle there is what holds this
patch to six hunks in one file.

Both getters fall through to the unmodified KeyChain path when no in-memory
entry is present: a hand-imported profile with no embedded PKCS#12 is a
legitimate upstream shape and must keep working.

Deliberately adds no VpnProfile column and does not touch DATABASE_VERSION.
Patch 0001 already squats version 20 while upstream is at 19; since the
migration gate is `column.Since > oldVersion`, a user already on our 20 would
never receive upstream's future since=20 columns. That pre-existing bug is not
deepened here -- an in-memory holder needs no schema at all.

The credential map is emptied here too, but ONLY on a real teardown
(mNextProfile == null). On a profile switch IpSecTunnel has already put() the
incoming identity, and a reconnect of the same profile reuses its UUID -- so
clearing unconditionally would delete the entry the snapshot is about to read
and drop the connect through to an absent KeyChain alias.

--- a/src/frontends/android/app/src/main/java/org/strongswan/android/logic/CharonVpnService.java
+++ b/src/frontends/android/app/src/main/java/org/strongswan/android/logic/CharonVpnService.java
@@ -100,6 +100,23 @@
 	private VpnProfile mCurrentProfile;
 	private volatile String mCurrentCertificateAlias;
 	private volatile String mCurrentUserCertificateAlias;
+	/* Non-null only for profiles whose client identity was parsed in-process
+	 * from an embedded PKCS#12 instead of installed into the Android KeyChain.
+	 * Read from charon's threads, so volatile like the aliases above. */
+	private volatile PrivycsCredentials.Entry mCurrentPrivycsCredentials;
+
+	/* Presence of this field is how the app proves at runtime that the vendor
+	 * patches were applied to this build. Without it an unpatched
+	 * CharonVpnService still compiles and merely falls back to the KeyChain
+	 * path, i.e. it fails as a mid-handshake auth error rather than as a build
+	 * error.
+	 *
+	 * MUST be read via getDeclaredField, never as a direct source reference:
+	 * javac inlines a static final String constant into the caller's own class
+	 * file, so a direct reference would still hold the literal against an
+	 * unpatched tree and the check would pass vacuously. Renaming it breaks
+	 * that reflective lookup silently. */
+	public static final String PRIVYCS_PATCH_MARKER = "privycs-inmemory-ipsec-identity";
 	private VpnProfile mNextProfile;
 	private volatile boolean mProfileUpdated;
 	private volatile boolean mTerminate;
@@ -287,6 +304,9 @@
 						 * a possible deadlock during deinitialization */
 						mCurrentCertificateAlias = mCurrentProfile.getCertificateAlias();
 						mCurrentUserCertificateAlias = mCurrentProfile.getUserCertificateAlias();
+						/* snapshotted here for the same reason as the aliases: the
+						 * lookup must not run on charon's thread during deinit */
+						mCurrentPrivycsCredentials = PrivycsCredentials.get(mCurrentProfile.getUUID());
 
 						startConnection(mCurrentProfile);
 						mIsDisconnecting = false;
@@ -339,6 +359,14 @@
 							setError(ErrorState.GENERIC_ERROR);
 							setState(State.DISABLED);
 							mCurrentProfile = null;
+							/* Second release edge, see stopCurrentConnection().
+							 * This branch parks the loop back in wait() with no
+							 * charon running, and stopCurrentConnection() cannot
+							 * cover it -- its teardown is gated on
+							 * mCurrentProfile != null, which this branch just
+							 * cleared. Without this the key would be held until
+							 * the next connect overwrote it. */
+							mCurrentPrivycsCredentials = null;
 						}
 					}
 				}
@@ -371,9 +399,30 @@
 				SimpleFetcher.disable();
 				deinitializeCharon();
 				Log.i(TAG, "charon stopped");
+				VpnProfile stopped = mCurrentProfile;
 				mCurrentProfile = null;
+				/* Released on the same edge as the profile it belongs to.
+				 * PrivycsCredentials.clear() only empties that class's map; this
+				 * field is a second strong reference, so without this line the
+				 * PrivateKey stays reachable for the whole service lifetime and
+				 * the "in-memory, released on disconnect" property this design
+				 * rests on would not hold. The alias fields above are left
+				 * dangling by upstream and we match that everywhere else -- a
+				 * stale String is inert, a live key is not.
+				 *
+				 * Must come after deinitializeCharon() returns: until it does,
+				 * charon's threads may still call getUserKey(). */
+				mCurrentPrivycsCredentials = null;
 				if (mNextProfile == null)
 				{	/* only do this if we are not connecting to another profile */
+					/* Drop the map entry too, so no strong reference to the key
+					 * outlives the tunnel. Deliberately NOT done when switching
+					 * profiles: IpSecTunnel has already put() the incoming
+					 * profile's identity by now, and a reconnect of the SAME
+					 * profile reuses its UUID -- clearing here would delete the
+					 * entry the snapshot below is about to read, and the connect
+					 * would fall through to the (absent) KeyChain alias. */
+					PrivycsCredentials.clear(stopped.getUUID());
 					removeNotification();
 					mBuilderAdapter.closeBlocking();
 				}
@@ -779,6 +828,13 @@
